reat this as a recommended process, not a claim that a particular automation guarantees an outcome.\n\nInclude a reusable brief template with fields for topic, audience, reader need, objective, scope and exclusions, approved sources, evidence and rights requirements, voice, deliverables, owner, review gates and version IDs. Include illustrative drafting and reviewer prompts that restrict the model to the approved materials, require citations or explicit evidence gaps, and prohibit invented quotations or evidence. Include a clearly labeled hypothetical tracker showing an item moving through brief, source set, draft, review, channel assets, package and human decision, with version identifiers and a blocked unsupported claim. Provide a practical checklist covering completeness, claim-by-claim verification, quotations, links, rights, versions and human approval. Do not include internal production instructions in public copy.\n\nEvidence and constraints\nOnly the three approved Google URLs in sources.json may support factual claims. The register contains candidate propositions for a reviewer to test; it does not establish that any source says them. No network sources were fetched or checked by this writer. Do not use a candidate proposition in the article as fact until the reviewer verifies its precise wording, context and current source version. Remove or revise unsupported propositions. Verify every factual claim, quotation and citation; preserve exact quotations only when independently checked. Record source access date, title, relevant passage or locator, and a version or snapshot identifier at the source gate. Block missing evidence or rights rather than filling gaps by inference.\n\nSearch demand, target site, market, conversion data and Search Console data are unavailable. Do not infer them. Avoid unsupported traffic, ranking, savings and algorithm claims, and do not import claims from other sources. The article may present the workflow and hypothetical tracker as recommendations and examples, clearly labeled as such.\n\nSuggested structure\n1. Set expectations: what AI can assist with and where human accountability remains.\n2. Choose an idea and complete the bounded brief and approved source set.\n3. Draft from the approved material using the sample prompt.\n4. Verify claims, quotations, citations and rights; show the sample reviewer prompt and blocking conditions.\n5. Track versions through the hypothetical example and human decision.\n6. Give the reusable template and final checklist.\n\nDownstream package and gates\nUse the complete plan-seo-geo-growth skill for the canonical draft and independent review when available. The skill file was not available at its listed local path in this writer's environment; the downstream writer/reviewer must locate and apply it. Prepare separate LinkedIn, X, Facebook, Instagram and Reddit drafts grounded in the approved article. Destinations are undecided; missing destination and current platform evidence block affected channels. Reddit remains blocked without a specified community and current community rules.\n\nAfter article approval, create an original editable OpenFilm workflow diagram and a 1080 \u00d7 1080 PNG candidate LinkedIn feed asset using exact excerpts from that approved article and code-drawn shapes only. No external images, music, footage or logos. Check rights and current actual platform requirements, record gaps, and do not label the candidate approved media. Assemble article, channel drafts, editable project, rendered asset, review findings, version lineage and human decision for a person. No publishing, scheduling, deployment, spending or credential creation is authorized by this brief.\n\nHandoff acceptance\nThis stage delivers the brief and candidate register only. A separate reviewer fetches approved URLs, verifies each proposed claim and all eventual article claims, records source versions and resolves or flags gaps. A passed brief stage does not imply the source gate, article, channels or media have passed.\nEOF\ncat > /var/home/user/Documents/agent-workflows/data/marketing-tools38/local-outputs/agent-workflows-bd5e41ba5cb9bc4b583ac147d21c576932c0f221f09cd129.scope/sources.json <<'EOF'\n{\n  \\\"claims\\\": [\n    {\\\"id\\\":\\\"C1\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"Candidate for verification: Google recommends creating helpful, reliable content intended primarily for people.\\\",\\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"S1\\\"]},\n    {\\\"id\\\":\\\"C2\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"Candidate for verification: Google provides guidance on using generative AI to create content and cautions about scaled content created primarily to manipulate search rankings.\\\",\\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"S2\\\"]},\n    {\\\"id\\\":\\\"C3\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"Candidate for verification: Google provides guidance on preparing content for AI features in Search.\\\",\\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"S3\\\"]}\n  ],\n  \\\"sources\\\": [\n    {\\\"id\\\":\\\"S1\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/creating-helpful-content\\\",\\\"evidence_id\\\":\\\"E1-pending-fetch\\\"},\n    {\\\"id\\\":\\\"S2\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/using-gen-ai-content\\\",\\\"evidence_id\\\":\\\"E2-pending-fetch\\\"},\n    {\\\"id\\\":\\\"S3\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/ai-optimization-guide\\\",\\\"evidence_id\\\":\\\"E3-pending-fetch\\\"}\n  ]\n}\nEOF\nsha256sum /var/home/user/Documents/agent-workflows/data/marketing-tools38/local-outputs/agent-workflows-bd5e41ba5cb9bc4b583ac147d21c576932c0f221f09cd129.scope/result.txt /var/home/user/Documents/agent-workflows/data/marketing-tools38/local-outputs/agent-workflows-bd5e41ba5cb9bc4b583ac147d21c576932c0f221f09cd129.scope/sources.json\npython -m json.tool /var/home/user/Documents/agent-workflows/data/marketing-tools38/local-outputs/agent-workflows-bd5e41ba5cb9bc4b583ac147d21c576932c0f221f09cd129.scope/sources.json >/dev/null\"","commandActions":[